1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,811
A tea strainer keeps the leaves back.

2
00:00:02,811 --> 00:00:04,787
Sea water dries to leave salt.

3
00:00:05,187 --> 00:00:08,838
A naphthalene ball in the
cupboard shrinks, but never melts.

4
00:00:09,288 --> 00:00:11,550
Diya watches her mother strain the tea.

5
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:13,364
Each trick uses one difference.

6
00:00:13,714 --> 00:00:14,614
Size.

7
00:00:14,192 --> 00:00:15,092
Drying.

8
00:00:14,756 --> 00:00:15,656
Vapour.

9
00:00:15,819 --> 00:00:17,962
Chemists clean compounds the same way.

10
00:00:17,962 --> 00:00:21,350
By the end, you will pick the
right trick for any mixture.

11
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,813
A chemist makes a new compound.

12
00:00:23,813 --> 00:00:25,542
It comes out mixed with junk.

13
00:00:25,892 --> 00:00:27,962
Pure means one substance only.

14
00:00:27,962 --> 00:00:30,294
So find one property that differs.

15
00:00:30,694 --> 00:00:31,784
Turns to vapour?

16
00:00:31,784 --> 00:00:32,684
Sublimation.

17
00:00:32,591 --> 00:00:33,903
Dissolves better hot?

18
00:00:33,903 --> 00:00:34,887
Crystallisation.

19
00:00:34,887 --> 00:00:36,376
Different boiling points?

20
00:00:36,376 --> 00:00:37,276
Distillation.

21
00:00:37,227 --> 00:00:38,628
Prefers another liquid?

22
00:00:38,628 --> 00:00:39,528
Extraction.

23
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:40,560
Sticks differently?

24
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:41,500
Chromatography.

25
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:43,571
Start with sublimation.

26
00:00:43,571 --> 00:00:47,008
A solid turns straight into
vapour, skipping the liquid.

27
00:00:47,358 --> 00:00:49,492
Camphor and naphthalene do this.

28
00:00:49,492 --> 00:00:50,392
Heat it.

29
00:00:50,256 --> 00:00:54,294
The vapour hits a cold surface
and turns back into pure solid.

30
00:00:54,294 --> 00:00:55,564
Junk stays below.

31
00:00:55,964 --> 00:00:58,189
Crystallisation is for solids too.

32
00:00:58,189 --> 00:01:01,443
Dissolve the solid in a
hot liquid, the solvent.

33
00:01:01,843 --> 00:01:02,751
Filter it hot.

34
00:01:02,751 --> 00:01:04,857
Then cool it, and pure crystals grow.

35
00:01:04,857 --> 00:01:06,247
Impurities stay dissolved.

36
00:01:06,647 --> 00:01:07,547
Coloured junk?

37
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:09,205
Activated charcoal soaks it up.

38
00:01:09,205 --> 00:01:10,105
Not pure yet?

39
00:01:10,077 --> 00:01:10,977
Repeat.

40
00:01:11,210 --> 00:01:14,247
Let's run a distillation
on our molecule bench.

41
00:01:14,247 --> 00:01:17,283
Boil, then cool the
vapour back into liquid.

42
00:01:17,633 --> 00:01:20,213
In the flask, chloroform and aniline.

43
00:01:20,213 --> 00:01:24,869
Chloroform boils at three hundred thirty
four kelvin, aniline at four fifty

44
00:01:24,869 --> 00:01:25,769
seven.

45
00:01:25,884 --> 00:01:26,784
We heat.

46
00:01:26,669 --> 00:01:28,280
Chloroform boils first.

47
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:32,103
Its vapour enters the condenser,
a tube cooled by water.

48
00:01:32,503 --> 00:01:35,959
It turns back into liquid
and drips into the receiver.

49
00:01:35,959 --> 00:01:37,809
Aniline stays in the flask.

50
00:01:38,209 --> 00:01:41,300
Boiling points far apart,
one round is enough.

51
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:43,166
This is simple distillation.

52
00:01:43,766 --> 00:01:45,211
Boiling points close?

53
00:01:45,211 --> 00:01:46,997
Use fractional distillation.

54
00:01:47,347 --> 00:01:50,095
A tall fractionating
column sits on the flask.

55
00:01:50,095 --> 00:01:53,586
The vapour cools and boils
again and again, getting purer.

56
00:01:53,586 --> 00:01:55,409
Crude oil is split this way.

57
00:01:55,809 --> 00:01:58,214
Glycerol breaks down before it boils.

58
00:01:58,214 --> 00:01:59,974
So we lower the pressure.

59
00:01:59,974 --> 00:02:02,369
Lower pressure, lower boiling point.

60
00:02:02,369 --> 00:02:05,586
Soap makers take glycerol
from spent lye this way.

61
00:02:05,986 --> 00:02:07,562
Now steam distillation.

62
00:02:07,562 --> 00:02:12,300
It is for a liquid that does not mix
with water, but moves with steam.

63
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:13,886
Like aniline in water.

64
00:02:14,236 --> 00:02:15,136
Step one.

65
00:02:14,984 --> 00:02:17,421
Each liquid has its own vapour pressure.

66
00:02:17,421 --> 00:02:18,690
Aniline's is p one.

67
00:02:18,690 --> 00:02:19,867
Water's is p two.

68
00:02:20,617 --> 00:02:21,517
Step two.

69
00:02:21,494 --> 00:02:26,352
The mixture boils when p one plus
p two equals the air pressure, p.

70
00:02:27,102 --> 00:02:28,027
Step three.

71
00:02:28,027 --> 00:02:29,908
So p one is less than p.

72
00:02:29,908 --> 00:02:33,007
Aniline comes over below
its own boiling point.

73
00:02:33,757 --> 00:02:35,451
Next, differential extraction.

74
00:02:35,451 --> 00:02:37,421
The compound is dissolved in water.

75
00:02:37,771 --> 00:02:42,267
Add a liquid that does not mix with
water, but dissolves the compound better.

76
00:02:42,267 --> 00:02:44,051
Shake in a separating funnel.

77
00:02:44,451 --> 00:02:46,914
The compound moves into the new layer.

78
00:02:46,914 --> 00:02:49,761
Drain the water, then
evaporate the solvent.

79
00:02:50,361 --> 00:02:51,569
Now chromatography.

80
00:02:51,569 --> 00:02:52,877
Chroma means colour.

81
00:02:53,227 --> 00:02:55,243
A stationary phase stays still.

82
00:02:55,243 --> 00:02:57,221
A mobile phase moves over it.

83
00:02:57,621 --> 00:03:02,097
In adsorption chromatography, the
still part is silica gel or alumina.

84
00:03:02,097 --> 00:03:04,181
What sticks more, moves less.

85
00:03:04,181 --> 00:03:06,999
Use a column, or a thin layer on glass.

86
00:03:07,399 --> 00:03:12,364
In paper chromatography, water held
in the paper is the stationary phase.

87
00:03:12,364 --> 00:03:14,615
This is partition chromatography.

88
00:03:15,015 --> 00:03:16,153
Watch the plate.

89
00:03:16,153 --> 00:03:19,767
The solvent climbs, and each
spot climbs at its own speed.

90
00:03:20,167 --> 00:03:24,754
R f is the distance moved by the
spot, divided by the distance moved by

91
00:03:24,754 --> 00:03:25,721
the solvent.

92
00:03:25,721 --> 00:03:27,465
Both from the base line.

93
00:03:27,865 --> 00:03:29,026
Colourless spots?

94
00:03:29,026 --> 00:03:31,461
Use ultraviolet light or iodine vapour.

95
00:03:31,461 --> 00:03:33,706
Amino acids show up with ninhydrin.

96
00:03:34,306 --> 00:03:35,206
Compare.

97
00:03:34,974 --> 00:03:36,506
Chloroform with aniline.

98
00:03:36,506 --> 00:03:39,646
They mix, and their boiling
points are far apart.

99
00:03:39,646 --> 00:03:40,934
Simple distillation.

100
00:03:41,334 --> 00:03:42,679
Aniline with water.

101
00:03:42,679 --> 00:03:45,788
They do not mix, and
aniline moves with steam.

102
00:03:45,788 --> 00:03:47,075
Steam distillation.

103
00:03:47,575 --> 00:03:48,688
Pause and think.

104
00:03:48,688 --> 00:03:51,765
Why not just boil aniline
at its own boiling point?

105
00:03:54,965 --> 00:03:57,082
It can break down at that heat.

106
00:03:57,082 --> 00:04:00,018
With steam, it comes
over cooler, and pure.

107
00:04:00,668 --> 00:04:01,621
Remember this.

108
00:04:01,621 --> 00:04:05,700
Find the one property that differs,
and pick the method that uses it.

109
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:08,280
Now, one worked example.

110
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:10,161
These are example figures.

111
00:04:10,461 --> 00:04:13,657
The solvent moves ten
centimetres from the base line.

112
00:04:13,657 --> 00:04:16,529
Spot A moves seven point
five, spot B three.

113
00:04:16,529 --> 00:04:17,634
Find each R f.

114
00:04:18,134 --> 00:04:19,034
Spot A.

115
00:04:18,857 --> 00:04:21,075
Seven point five divided by ten.

116
00:04:21,075 --> 00:04:22,891
R f is point seven five.

117
00:04:23,641 --> 00:04:24,541
Spot B.

118
00:04:24,415 --> 00:04:26,071
Three divided by ten.

119
00:04:26,071 --> 00:04:27,684
R f is point three.

120
00:04:28,434 --> 00:04:31,630
B sticks more to the
plate, so it moved less.

121
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:33,741
Now, the exam trap.

122
00:04:34,091 --> 00:04:37,688
Students measure from the plate
edge, or divide solvent by spot.

123
00:04:38,088 --> 00:04:41,127
Measure both from the
base line, spot on top.

124
00:04:41,127 --> 00:04:43,081
R f is never more than one.

125
00:04:43,731 --> 00:04:45,189
Where is this asked?

126
00:04:45,189 --> 00:04:49,102
JEE Main has asked to match
mixtures with distillation methods.

127
00:04:49,102 --> 00:04:50,667
Boards ask about R f.

128
00:04:51,267 --> 00:04:52,408
Exam level now.

129
00:04:52,408 --> 00:04:56,083
The solvent front is twelve
centimetres from the base line.

130
00:04:56,083 --> 00:04:57,988
Spot X has R f point four.

131
00:04:57,988 --> 00:05:00,977
Spot Y is three point
six centimetres above X.

132
00:05:00,977 --> 00:05:02,437
Find the R f of Y.

133
00:05:02,437 --> 00:05:03,626
Pause and solve.

134
00:05:09,026 --> 00:05:10,374
The answer is A.

135
00:05:10,374 --> 00:05:11,387
Point seven.

136
00:05:11,887 --> 00:05:14,298
Spot X moved point four times twelve.

137
00:05:14,298 --> 00:05:15,523
Four point eight.

138
00:05:16,023 --> 00:05:19,366
Spot Y moved four point
eight plus three point six.

139
00:05:19,366 --> 00:05:20,625
Eight point four.

140
00:05:21,125 --> 00:05:24,049
Eight point four over
twelve is point seven.

141
00:05:24,049 --> 00:05:26,055
B used only three point six.

142
00:05:26,655 --> 00:05:28,195
Here is your recap.

143
00:05:28,195 --> 00:05:29,778
Screenshot this card.

144
00:05:32,978 --> 00:05:35,164
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

145
00:05:35,564 --> 00:05:36,464
One.

146
00:05:35,939 --> 00:05:37,404
Which method purifies camphor?

147
00:05:38,204 --> 00:05:39,104
Two.

148
00:05:38,645 --> 00:05:41,790
Why does steam distillation
work below the boiling point?

149
00:05:42,590 --> 00:05:43,490
Three.

150
00:05:43,154 --> 00:05:46,279
What is the stationary phase
in paper chromatography?

151
00:05:47,179 --> 00:05:51,689
Next time: how can we prove nitrogen
is hiding inside a compound?

152
00:05:51,689 --> 00:05:54,205
We find out in qualitative analysis.
